<Base
  title="About · Ryunosuke O'Neil"
  description="Software engineer at CERN working on distributed data processing for the LHCb experiment; formerly a PhD student at Edinburgh."
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    I'm a software engineer at CERN, in the LHCb Computing Section, working on the
    distributed data-processing infrastructure and software stack that
    supports the <a href="http://lhcb-public.web.cern.ch">LHCb experiment</a> and
    its physicists. In practice that means the <a
      href="/projects/#analysis-productions">Analysis Productions</a> platform,
    CWL-based workflows for the <a href="https://diracx.diracgrid.org/en/latest/">DiracX grid middleware</a>, and the
    operations tooling that keeps thousands of grid jobs running concurrently.
  </p>
  <figure class="about-photo">
    <img
      src="/images/chep2026-bangkok.jpg"
      alt="Ryunosuke O'Neil presenting at CHEP 2026 in Bangkok, holding a microphone in front of a session backdrop."
      width="900"
      height="805"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>Presenting at CHEP 2026</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    I came to software engineering through physics. During my PhD in the <a
      href="https://www.ph.ed.ac.uk/particle-physics-experiment"
      >Particle Physics Experiment group</a
    > at the University of Edinburgh, I led a search for excited charmed
    baryons over LHCb's Run 2 dataset, which resulted in the observation of a
    new state, the Ξ<sub>c</sub>(2923)<sup>+</sup>, published in <a
      href="https://inspirehep.net/literature/2894871">Physical Review Letters</a>. Producing the n-tuples for
    that analysis made me an
    intensive user of the very tooling I would later rebuild: alongside the
    physics, I created <a href="/projects/#lbapweb">LbAPWeb</a>, the web
    application LHCb analysts now use to create and manage their datasets.
    The motivation came directly from knowing, first-hand, where the
    frustrations were.
  </p>

  <p>
    Earlier, I spent time closer to the detectors: two testbeam campaigns at
    the DESY-II facility in 2022, evaluating irradiated HV-MAPS sensors for
    the LHCb MightyPix project, and before that a Masters at the University of
    Manchester on data-driven alignment of the tracker for the <a
      href="https://mu2e.fnal.gov">Mu2e experiment</a> at <a
      href="https://fnal.gov">Fermilab</a>, using cosmic ray simulations to test it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of my work happens in the open; You can see what I've been up to recently on my <a
      href="https://github.com/ryuwd">GitHub</a> and <a
      href="https://gitlab.cern.ch/roneil">CERN GitLab</a>.
  </p>

